<quiz>
  <question type="aitext">
    <exampledescription>Find the short bolt in a flange joint image and explain the risk. Shows a labelled image (bolts A to D) so answers can be specific.</exampledescription>
    <name>
      <text>Mechanical inspection: bolted flange joint</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/flange_short_bolt.svg" alt="Side view of a bolted pipe flange joint with four bolts labelled A to D" width="660" height="440"></p>
<p>The image shows a bolted pipe flange joint that has just been assembled. Examine it carefully. Identify any assembly error visible, say which bolt (A to D) is affected, and explain why it is incorrect and what could happen in service.</p>]]></text>
      <file name="flange_short_bolt.svg" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Bolt C is too short: its end does not protrude beyond the nut, whereas bolts A, B and D show two to three threads past the nut. A bolt end should project at least one to two full threads beyond the nut to ensure full thread engagement. With partial engagement, fewer threads carry the load, so they can strip under the bolt preload or line pressure. Bolt C may also not be clamped to the same preload as the others, giving uneven gasket compression and a risk of leaks or joint failure. Bolt C should be replaced with a bolt of the correct length.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>10</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber>engin-bolted-flange-joint</idnumber>
    <responseformat>plain</responseformat>
    <responsefieldlines>6</responsefieldlines>
    <minwordlimit/>
    <maxwordlimit>200</maxwordlimit>
    <graderinfo format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Expected answer: bolt C is too short / does not protrude through the nut, so there is insufficient (partial) thread engagement. Consequence: threads may strip, preload is reduced or uneven, gasket compression is uneven, leading to a leak or joint failure.</p>]]></text>
    </graderinfo>
    <responsetemplate format="html">
      <text/>
    </responsetemplate>
    <aiprompt>You cannot see the image the student was shown, so use this description of it: a side view of two pipe flanges with a gasket between them, joined by four hex bolts labelled A, B, C and D from left to right. Each bolt has a washer under the head (above the top flange) and a washer and hex nut below the bottom flange. On bolts A, B and D the threaded end of the bolt projects two to three threads beyond the nut. On bolt C no bolt end is visible below the nut, because the bolt is too short and finishes inside the nut. Everything else (washers, gasket, nuts, pipe) is correct and is not the fault.
Check whether the student identifies that bolt C is too short and does not fully engage the nut (insufficient thread engagement, bolt end not protruding past the nut). They should then explain the consequence: fewer threads carry the load so the threads can strip, the bolt may not hold its preload, the gasket may be unevenly compressed, and the joint may leak or fail under pressure. Mention the general rule that at least one to two full threads should protrude beyond the nut if the student does not.</aiprompt>
    <markscheme>10 marks: identifies bolt C, states that it is too short / does not protrude through the nut (insufficient thread engagement), AND explains a valid consequence (stripped threads, loss of preload, uneven gasket compression, leakage or joint failure).
6 marks: identifies the fault on bolt C correctly but gives no consequence, or gives only a vague one (e.g. "it is not safe").
3 marks: notices that one bolt looks different from the others but does not identify the problem correctly or names the wrong bolt while describing the right fault.
0 marks: does not identify the short bolt, or names an error that is not present (e.g. missing washer, wrong gasket).</markscheme>
    <model/>
    <spellcheck>0</spellcheck>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>Bolt C is too short. On A, B and D the thread sticks out a couple of threads below the nut, but on C it doesn't come through at all, so the nut is only partly engaged. With fewer threads carrying the load the threads could strip when torqued or under pressure, and the gasket won't be clamped evenly, so the flange could leak.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>Bolt C does not come all the way through the nut.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>Bolt C looks different to the others, maybe the nut is the wrong size.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>There is no washer under the nut on bolt B so it will loosen with vibration.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
